  "limitations": [
    "Official paper describes a final output of 6, but 25-28% of audited request groups contain more than 6 unique exposed videos. Same user/time and zero request/video duplicates do not explain this discrepancy. Do not invent causes or silently truncate to claim original OneRec batching.",
    "Most repeated ordering values are zero. A tie-break by video_id or file order defines a surrogate order, not recovered final display order.",
    "Zero duration occurs in 8-10% of rows and over half of Q request groups. It does not invalidate positive playing_time automatically; silently removing these requests substantially changes the population.",
    "No final display order was verified; rank_index and rerank_index refer to intermediate system stages.",
    "Potential future duplicate request/video rows would inflate played/effective/summed time because the reviewed script only drops exact full-row duplicates. This does not affect these four audited files, where pair duplicates are zero.",
    "The reviewed extraction main enumerates all existing .feather files in output directories, not only current complete_members. Fresh or manifest-selected inputs are required to avoid stale files in reruns. No stale input was found in this review.",
    "The first 3 downloaded days are a short chronological window; their cold-item fractions cannot be generalized to the full 37-day train/test split.",
    "The complete archive has not been verified; the local complete members have hashes and readable Feather structures.",
    "A manual logged-request list task can be explicitly defined, but strict replication of OneRec returned/watch list semantics remains unverified."
  ],
